<PaperLayout
  title="Improving Language Understanding by Generative Pre-Training"
  eyebrow="NLP · GPT-1 · 2018 / OpenAI"
  authors="Alec Radford · Karthik Narasimhan · Tim Salimans · Ilya Sutskever"
  arxivUrl="https://cdn.openai.com/research-covers/language-unsupervised/language_understanding_paper.pdf"
  subtitle="A structured reading of the paper that turned generative language-model pretraining into a general recipe for natural language understanding."
  sections={sections}
  annotations={annotations}
  initialAnnotation="generativePretraining"
  compactTitle={true}
>
  <section id="overview" class="reading-section">
    <h2>Overview</h2>

    <div class="guide-box">
      <strong>Reading guide</strong>
      <p>
        Read GPT-1 as a paper about a <em>change in interface</em>. Before this line of work, many NLP systems were built
        around task-specific architectures: one design for entailment, another for question answering, another for sentiment,
        another for paraphrase detection. GPT-1 proposes a different center of gravity: first train one large language model
        on raw text, then adapt many tasks to that model.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      The paper's headline idea is now familiar: use <button class="anno" data-anno="generativePretraining">generative pre-training</button>
      on unlabeled text, then use <button class="anno" data-anno="discriminativeFineTuning">discriminative fine-tuning</button>
      on labeled tasks. But in 2018, this was not yet the default NLP recipe. The default mental model was still closer to:
      build a good supervised model for a specific dataset, perhaps initialize it with pretrained word embeddings, and tune the
      architecture around that task.
    </p>

    <p>
      GPT-1 changes that story. Its question is not simply, "Can we improve one benchmark?" It asks whether a single
      <button class="anno" data-anno="decoderOnly">decoder-only Transformer</button> language model, trained only to predict
      the next token, can become a general backbone for <button class="anno" data-anno="nlu">natural language understanding</button>.
    </p>

    <div class="architecture-flow history-flow">
      <span>Unlabeled books</span>
      <span>→</span>
      <span>Generative LM pretraining</span>
      <span>→</span>
      <span>Task input transformation</span>
      <span>→</span>
      <span>Supervised fine-tuning</span>
    </div>

    <p>
      This is why the paper is a bridge between classic NLP and large language models. It still evaluates on classic NLU
      benchmarks such as GLUE, RACE, SNLI, and CoLA. But the organizing idea is already the LLM idea: language modeling on
      large-scale text can produce a reusable base model, and downstream tasks should be expressed through that base model's
      text interface.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="why" class="reading-section">
    <h2>Why this paper appeared</h2>

    <p>
      GPT-1 did not appear from nowhere. It was the meeting point of several earlier observations. The paper should be read
      as a synthesis: word embeddings had shown that unlabeled text was useful; semi-supervised pretraining had shown that
      unsupervised objectives could improve supervised learning; OpenAI's sentiment-neuron work suggested that language
      modeling could learn surprisingly high-level semantic features; and the Transformer provided an architecture that could
      carry these ideas at a larger scale.
    </p>

    <div class="results-table-wrap">
      <table class="results-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Precursor</th>
            <th>What it already showed</th>
            <th>What was still missing</th>
            <th>How GPT-1 uses it</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {historicalRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.source}</td>
              <td>{row.before}</td>
              <td>{row.limitation}</td>
              <td>{row.role}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      The most story-like precursor is the <button class="anno" data-anno="sentimentNeuron">sentiment neuron</button>.
      OpenAI had trained a language model to predict the next character in Amazon reviews and found that the model developed
      a feature strongly aligned with sentiment. The important lesson was not merely that sentiment analysis worked. The lesson
      was that prediction-only training could create semantic structure inside the model. See OpenAI's
      <a
        href="https://openai.com/index/unsupervised-sentiment-neuron/"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noreferrer"
        style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; font-weight: 600;"
      >
        Unsupervised sentiment neuron ↗
      </a>
      blog post.
    </p>

    <p>
      GPT-1 turns that clue into a broader program. If a language model can learn sentiment while only predicting text, maybe
      a larger Transformer language model trained on long books can learn many kinds of linguistic and world knowledge: syntax,
      coreference, entailment, discourse, commonsense, and answer plausibility. OpenAI's own GPT-1 blog explicitly frames the
      work as following from the sentiment-neuron result; see
      <a
        href="https://openai.com/index/language-unsupervised/"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noreferrer"
        style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; font-weight: 600;"
      >
        Improving language understanding with unsupervised learning ↗
      </a>.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">Where GPT-1 sits</span>
      <p>
        GPT-1 sits at a fork in the post-Transformer NLP timeline. Transformer made large attention-based language models
        practical. GPT-1 then explored the causal, decoder-only branch: train a model to predict the next token, and reuse
        the same model for language understanding through fine-tuning. BERT later pushed a different branch: bidirectional,
        encoder-only pretraining designed especially for NLU. This contrast makes GPT-1 easier to place historically:
        it is the generative pretraining route that later leads naturally to GPT-2, GPT-3, and modern decoder-only LLMs.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="thesis" class="reading-section">
    <h2>The paper's thesis</h2>

    <p>
      The paper begins from a data asymmetry. Labeled task datasets are small and expensive, but raw text is abundant. A
      purely supervised NLU model must learn both language and task behavior from limited annotations. GPT-1 separates these
      two jobs. First, learn broad language knowledge from raw text. Then, use labeled data only to teach the model how to map
      that knowledge into a task-specific decision.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">The central claim</span>
      <p>
        Large gains on language-understanding tasks can be achieved by first training a language model on a diverse unlabeled
        corpus and then fine-tuning it on each supervised task, while making only minimal changes to the model architecture.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      This is a <button class="anno" data-anno="semiSupervised">semi-supervised learning</button> paper because it uses both
      unlabeled and labeled data. But its deeper claim is about representation and transfer. The authors do not want a feature
      extractor that gives word vectors to many separate systems. They want the full pretrained neural network to become the
      reusable object.
    </p>

    <div class="concept-grid">
      <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>Old center</h3>
        <p>
          The task architecture is central. Pretrained features, if used, are added to a supervised system designed for one task.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>GPT-1 center</h3>
        <p>
          The pretrained language model is central. Tasks are converted into token sequences that the same model can read.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-card">
        <h3>Later LLM center</h3>
        <p>
          The model interface becomes natural language itself. Many tasks are handled by prompting, instruction tuning, or
          lightweight adaptation rather than separate architectures.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="method" class="reading-section">
    <h2>Method</h2>

    <p>
      The method has two main stages and one interface trick. The stages are pretraining and fine-tuning. The interface trick
      is <button class="anno" data-anno="inputTransformations">task-specific input transformation</button>: convert every task
      into a text sequence that a causal Transformer can process.
    </p>

    <div class="results-table-wrap">
      <table class="results-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Stage</th>
            <th>Data</th>
            <th>Objective</th>
            <th>What comes out</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {trainingStageRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.stage}</td>
              <td>{row.data}</td>
              <td>{row.objective}</td>
              <td>{row.output}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      The paper uses <button class="anno" data-anno="booksCorpus">BooksCorpus</button> for pretraining. That choice matters.
      Books are long, contiguous sequences, so the model can learn dependencies across many sentences. A shuffled sentence
      corpus can teach local language statistics, but it destroys many long-range structures that matter for narrative,
      discourse, reference, and commonsense reasoning.
    </p>

    <p>
      During fine-tuning, the paper adds only a small number of task-specific parameters: the output layer for the target
      labels and embeddings for special delimiter tokens. This is why the paper calls the model task-agnostic. The model is
      not completely identical across tasks, but the Transformer body is reused.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="math" class="reading-section">
    <h2>Mathematical core</h2>

    <p>
      The mathematical framework is easiest to understand if we separate <strong>training stages</strong> from
      <strong>training objectives</strong>. GPT-1 has two main stages: first, unsupervised language-model pretraining;
      second, supervised fine-tuning on downstream tasks. The paper writes these objectives as <code>L₁</code>,
      <code>L₂</code>, and <code>L₃</code>.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">Do not read L₁, L₂, L₃ as three separate models</span>
      <p>
        <code>L₁</code> is the language-model objective. <code>L₂</code> is the supervised task objective.
        <code>L₃</code> is not a third training stage; it is the fine-tuning objective when the authors add
        an auxiliary language-model term during supervised fine-tuning.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="results-table-wrap">
      <table class="results-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Objective</th>
            <th>Where it is used</th>
            <th>What it trains the model to do</th>
            <th>Intuition</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>L₁</code></td>
            <td>Pretraining; also reused as an auxiliary term during fine-tuning</td>
            <td>Predict the next token from previous tokens</td>
            <td>Learn general language structure from unlabeled text.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>L₂</code></td>
            <td>Supervised fine-tuning</td>
            <td>Predict the correct downstream task label</td>
            <td>Adapt the pretrained model to a specific NLU task.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>L₃</code></td>
            <td>Fine-tuning with an auxiliary objective</td>
            <td>Optimize the task label objective and the language-model objective together</td>
            <td>Learn the task while preserving general language-model ability.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>1. Language-model pretraining: L₁</h3>

    <p>
      The unlabeled corpus is treated as a long sequence of tokens:
    </p>

    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
      <code>U = &#123;u₁, u₂, ..., uₙ&#125;</code>
    </div>

    <p>
      GPT-1 trains the model to predict each token from the previous <code>k</code> tokens:
    </p>

    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
      <code>L₁(U) = Σᵢ log P(uᵢ | uᵢ₋ₖ, ..., uᵢ₋₁; Θ)</code>
    </div>

    <p>
      Here, <code>Θ</code> means all parameters of the Transformer language model. The objective is maximized,
      so the model is rewarded when it assigns high probability to the real next token. In ordinary deep-learning
      code, this is often implemented by minimizing the negative log-likelihood, but the idea is the same.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">Concrete example</span>
      <p>
        Suppose the text is <em>The movie was surprisingly good</em>. A language model learns to predict
        <code>movie</code> after seeing <code>The</code>, then <code>was</code> after seeing <code>The movie</code>,
        and so on. This looks simple, but to do it well across billions of words, the model must learn syntax,
        semantics, topic, style, entities, and long-range dependencies.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      This is the heart of <button class="anno" data-anno="generativePretraining">generative pretraining</button>:
      the model learns from raw text without task labels by predicting what comes next.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Transformer language model</h3>

    <p>
      Before the tokens enter the Transformer blocks, GPT-1 turns them into vectors. The initial hidden representation
      combines token embeddings and positional embeddings:
    </p>

    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
      <code>h₀ = U Wₑ + Wₚ</code>
    </div>

    <p>
      <code>Wₑ</code> is the token embedding matrix. It maps each token into a vector. <code>Wₚ</code> is the
      position embedding matrix. It tells the model where each token appears in the sequence. Position information
      is necessary because self-attention alone does not know whether a word came first, last, or in the middle.
    </p>

    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
      <code>hₗ = transformer_block(hₗ₋₁)</code>
    </div>

    <p>
      Each Transformer block applies masked self-attention and a feed-forward network. The mask is important:
      when predicting token <code>uᵢ</code>, the model can attend only to earlier tokens, not future tokens.
      This is why GPT-1 is a decoder-only, autoregressive language model.
    </p>

    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
      <code>P(u) = softmax(hₙ Wₑᵀ)</code>
    </div>

    <p>
      After the final Transformer layer, the hidden state is projected back to the vocabulary. The softmax produces
      a probability distribution over possible next tokens.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Supervised fine-tuning: L₂</h3>

    <p>
      After pretraining, the model knows how to model text, but it does not yet know the label space of a specific
      task. For a supervised dataset <code>C</code>, each example contains an input sequence
      <code>x¹, ..., xᵐ</code> and a label <code>y</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
      GPT-1 feeds the whole input sequence into the pretrained Transformer, takes the final hidden representation,
      and uses a new linear layer <code>Wᵧ</code> to predict the task label:
    </p>

    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
      <code>P(y | x¹, ..., xᵐ) = softmax(hₗᵐ Wᵧ)</code>
    </div>

    <p>
      The supervised fine-tuning objective is:
    </p>

    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
      <code>L₂(C) = Σ₍ₓ,ᵧ₎ log P(y | x¹, ..., xᵐ)</code>
    </div>

    <p>
      <code>L₂</code> rewards the model for assigning high probability to the correct label. For sentiment classification,
      the label might be <code>positive</code> or <code>negative</code>. For natural language inference, the label might be
      <code>entailment</code>, <code>contradiction</code>, or <code>neutral</code>.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">What changes during fine-tuning?</span>
      <p>
        GPT-1 does not train only the final classifier. The pretrained Transformer parameters are also updated.
        Pretraining gives the model a strong initialization; fine-tuning adapts that model to the labeled task.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>4. Fine-tuning with an auxiliary language-model objective: L₃</h3>

    <p>
      The authors also found it useful to keep a language-model objective during fine-tuning. This produces the
      combined objective:
    </p>

    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
      <code>L₃(C) = L₂(C) + λ L₁(C)</code>
    </div>

    <p>
      The main task objective is <code>L₂(C)</code>. The auxiliary term is <code>L₁(C)</code>, which means the model
      continues to do next-token prediction on the downstream task's input text. The coefficient <code>λ</code> is the weight
      that controls how strongly the auxiliary language-model objective influences fine-tuning.
    </p>

    <div class="concept-grid">
      <div class="concept-card">
        <h3><code>L₂(C)</code></h3>
        <p>
          The model learns the task label. For example, it learns that a given movie review should be classified as
          <code>positive</code>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="concept-card">
        <h3><code>λ L₁(C)</code></h3>
        <p>
          The model continues to model the task text as language. This provides token-level training signals and acts
          like a regularizer.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="concept-card">
        <h3><code>L₃(C)</code></h3>
        <p>
          The model learns the supervised task while being encouraged not to forget the language-modeling ability learned
          during pretraining.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">Why this term is optional</span>
      <p>
        The core GPT-1 recipe is pretrain with <code>L₁</code>, then fine-tune with <code>L₂</code>. The auxiliary
        language-model objective is an extra fine-tuning choice. In the paper's ablation study, removing it does not
        destroy performance, which shows that the main source of GPT-1's improvement is generative pretraining itself,
        not the auxiliary term.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      In one sentence: <code>L₁</code> teaches the model general language modeling, <code>L₂</code> teaches the model
      the downstream task, and <code>L₃</code> combines task learning with an auxiliary language-modeling signal during
      fine-tuning.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="decoder" class="reading-section">
    <h2>Why decoder-only is enough</h2>

    <p>
      A common question is: if GPT-1 is a decoder-only generative model, why can it do understanding tasks? The answer is that
      GPT-1 changes the task interface. A causal decoder can only look left, but if all the information needed for a decision is
      placed before a final decision position, that final position can attend to the entire input.
    </p>

    <div class="architecture-flow">
      <span>Task text</span>
      <span>+</span>
      <span>special separators</span>
      <span>+</span>
      <span>final extraction token</span>
      <span>→</span>
      <span>final hidden state sees all previous tokens</span>
      <span>→</span>
      <span>classification</span>
    </div>

    <p>
      In the original encoder-decoder Transformer, the encoder reads a source sequence and the decoder generates a target
      sequence. GPT-1 does not need the encoder because its pretraining target is not translation. It only needs an
      autoregressive model of text:
    </p>

    <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
      <code>P(x) = P(x₁) P(x₂ | x₁) P(x₃ | x₁, x₂) ... P(xₙ | x₍&lt;n₎)</code>
    </div>

    <p>
      The <button class="anno" data-anno="causalMask">causal mask</button> makes sure token <code>i</code> cannot attend to
      future tokens. This is exactly the constraint needed for next-token prediction. The same constraint does not prevent NLU
      fine-tuning, because the final extraction position is placed after the premise, hypothesis, question, document, or answer
      option.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">The key intuition</span>
      <p>
        Decoder-only is not saying the model only generates and cannot understand. It says the model's universal operation is
        causal sequence modeling. Understanding tasks become possible when their inputs are serialized into a sequence and the
        decision is made at the end.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      This is one of GPT-1's most important ideological moves. Rather than forcing the architecture to match every task's
      structure, the paper forces the task to match the model's sequence interface.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="architecture" class="reading-section">
    <h2>Architecture and task interface</h2>

    <p>
      GPT-1 uses one pretrained causal Transformer as the shared text-processing engine. The important design move is not to
      build a separate architecture for every NLU task. Instead, the paper rewrites each task into a token sequence that a
      decoder-only language model can read from left to right.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">The interface shift</span>
      <p>
        Instead of asking, <em>"What special model should we design for entailment, similarity, or QA?"</em> GPT-1 asks,
        <em>"How can we frame this task as a sequence so the same pretrained Transformer can solve it?"</em>
      </p>
    </div>

