  <section id="analysis" class="reading-section">
    <h2>Analysis</h2>

    <p>
      The strongest empirical result is not only that GPT-1 gets good benchmark scores. The deeper question is
      <strong>where the improvement comes from</strong>. Is the model better because it uses a Transformer? Because it keeps
      a language-modeling objective during fine-tuning? Or because unsupervised pretraining has already built useful linguistic
      machinery before the model ever sees task labels?
    </p>

    <p>
      The answer is that the model's ability is distributed across the pretrained Transformer itself. GPT-1 is not merely using
      pretrained word vectors. It transfers a whole stack of contextual computation: token representations, attention patterns,
      intermediate abstractions, and higher-level sequence features. This is why the paper matters historically. It moves NLP
      from <em>pretrained embeddings</em> toward <em>pretrained models</em>.
    </p>

    <h3>1. The whole pretrained model matters</h3>

    <p>
      Earlier NLP systems often transferred only word embeddings. That helped, but it meant the downstream model still had to
      learn most of the task-specific reasoning from labeled data. GPT-1 tests a more ambitious idea: after a language model has
      been trained on large amounts of text, every layer of the network may contain reusable language-processing ability.
    </p>

    <p>
      When more pretrained layers are transferred into downstream tasks, performance keeps improving. Transferring embeddings is
      useful, but transferring deeper Transformer blocks is even better. Full transfer gives the strongest result. The lesson is
      that the pretrained model has learned more than word meaning; it has learned contextual operations that downstream tasks can
      reuse.
    </p>

    <div class="architecture-flow">
      <span>Word embeddings</span>
      <span>→</span>
      <span>lower contextual layers</span>
      <span>→</span>
      <span>deeper Transformer blocks</span>
      <span>→</span>
      <span>full pretrained language model</span>
    </div>

    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">Why this matters</span>
      <p>
        GPT-1 changes the unit of transfer. The reusable object is no longer just a vector for each word. It is a trained
        sequence-processing system that can be adapted to classification, entailment, similarity, and multiple-choice reasoning.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>2. Task behavior appears before task training</h3>

    <p>
      GPT-1 is still mainly a fine-tuning paper. It is not yet a prompting paper in the later GPT-3 sense. But the authors notice
      something important: even before supervised fine-tuning, the pretrained language model already shows weak but meaningful
      task-relevant behavior.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is tested with simple heuristics. The model is not given a task-specific classifier. Instead, the researchers ask
      whether language-model probabilities themselves can be used as a crude decision rule. If a sentence is grammatical, the
      language model should assign it higher probability. If an answer makes sense after a passage and question, the model should
      assign that continuation higher probability. If a sentiment completion is more likely after a review, that probability can
      be treated as a sentiment signal.
    </p>

    <div class="results-table-wrap">
      <table class="results-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Task</th>
            <th>Zero-shot heuristic</th>
            <th>Why this makes sense for a language model</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {zeroShotRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.task}</td>
              <td>{row.heuristic}</td>
              <td>{row.intuition}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      These heuristic scores generally improve as language-model pretraining continues. That does not mean GPT-1 is already a
      strong zero-shot system. The more important point is that next-token prediction appears to create pieces of task behavior
      as a byproduct. The model learns patterns that look like grammar judgment, sentiment recognition, answer selection, and
      coreference resolution because those abilities help it predict text.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">The historical signal</span>
      <p>
        This is one of the bridges from GPT-1 to later GPT systems. GPT-1 still relies on fine-tuning, but it already suggests
        that sufficiently trained language models contain latent task abilities before explicit task training.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>3. What actually causes the improvement?</h3>

    <p>
      The ablations separate three possible explanations. Maybe a Transformer is already strong enough without pretraining.
      Maybe the auxiliary language-modeling objective during fine-tuning is the key. Or maybe the main source is the initial
      generative pretraining on unlabeled text.
    </p>

    <div class="results-table-wrap">
      <table class="results-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Variant</th>
            <th>Average score</th>
            <th>What it tells us</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {ablationRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.variant}</td>
              <td>{row.average}</td>
              <td>{row.meaning}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      The largest drop happens when pretraining is removed. A Transformer trained only on supervised task data performs far worse
      than the pretrained version. This shows that the architecture alone is not enough. The model needs the broad linguistic
      knowledge and strong initialization produced by language modeling on large unlabeled text.
    </p>

    <p>
      Replacing the Transformer with an LSTM also hurts, which supports the choice of self-attention. The LSTM can be pretrained,
      but it does not transfer as well. Long-range attention gives the Transformer a better way to store and reuse dependencies
      across tokens, sentences, and structured inputs.
    </p>

    <p>
      Removing the auxiliary language-modeling objective during fine-tuning is much less decisive. It helps some larger tasks,
      but it is not the main reason the system works. The central mechanism is still the same: pretrain a high-capacity causal
      Transformer on unlabeled text, then adapt the whole model to supervised tasks.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-passage">
      <span class="passage-label">The analysis takeaway</span>
      <p>
        The evidence points to a clear hierarchy. Generative pretraining is the main source of transfer. The Transformer is the
        right carrier for that transfer. The auxiliary language-modeling objective is a useful training detail, but not the core
        discovery.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="legacy" class="reading-section">
    <h2>Why it became a bridge</h2>

    <p>
      GPT-1 is still an NLP paper in its benchmarks and evaluation style. It fine-tunes on supervised datasets and reports
      task-specific scores. But it is already an LLM paper in its worldview: the most important artifact is the pretrained
      language model, and the most important training signal is large-scale text prediction.
    </p>

    <p>
      Its immediate legacy is the <strong>pretrain-then-fine-tune</strong> recipe. Its deeper legacy is the belief that next-token
      prediction is not merely a generation trick; it can be a general-purpose representation-learning objective. GPT-2 scaled
      this idea and emphasized zero-shot transfer more strongly. GPT-3 scaled it further and made in-context learning central.
      Scaling laws later studied how predictable improvements emerge as model size, data, and compute increase.
    </p>

    <div class="architecture-flow history-flow">
      <span>Word embeddings</span>
      <span>→</span>
      <span>Sentiment neuron</span>
      <span>→</span>
      <span>Transformer</span>
      <span>→</span>
      <span>GPT-1: pretrain + fine-tune</span>
      <span>→</span>
      <span>GPT-2: stronger zero-shot LM</span>
      <span>→</span>
      <span>GPT-3 / scaling</span>
    </div>

    <p>
      This is why GPT-1 is so useful in a classic-paper library. It is not only a model checkpoint or a benchmark result. It is
      a conceptual pivot: instead of treating language models as auxiliary tools for NLP systems, it treats the language model
      as the system that downstream NLP tasks should adapt to.
    </p>
  </section>

